---
import Base from '@/layouts/Base.astro';
import { GOATCOUNTER_CODE } from '@/lib/analytics';

const counting = GOATCOUNTER_CODE !== '';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description={
    counting
      ? 'Everything you enter stays in your own browser. No accounts, no cookies; only anonymous page counts. Here is exactly how that works.'
      : 'Ethereal collects nothing. No accounts, no cookies, no analytics. Your inputs stay in your own browser. Here is exactly how that works.'
  }
  path="/privacy/"
>
  <div class="container-page pt-12 pb-16 sm:pt-16">
    <article class="prose-ethereal max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl leading-[1.05] tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="text-lg">
        {counting
          ? 'Short version: nothing you enter ever leaves your browser, and we collect no personal data. We only count page views, anonymously, and you can switch even that off.'
          : 'Short version: we do not collect, store or transmit any personal data. There is nothing to opt out of.'}
      </p>
      <h2>What happens to what you type</h2>
      <p>
        Your profile, plan, weigh-ins, food diary, saved meals, preferences and weekly reflections
        are saved in your browser's <code>localStorage</code> under keys starting with{' '}
        <code>ethereal:</code>. Progress photos are stored in the browser's IndexedDB database named{' '}
        <code>ethereal-photos</code>, compressed on your device. None of it is ever sent to us or to
        anyone else; there is no account and no server that could receive it.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can export everything as a JSON backup, import it on another device, and delete all of
        it in one tap from Settings → Delete all my data, which also removes the photos. Clearing
        site data in your browser does the same.
      </p>
      <h2>Barcodes and product search</h2>
      <p>
        When you scan a barcode or search packaged products, your browser asks{' '}
        <a href="https://world.openfoodfacts.org" rel="noopener">
          Open Food Facts
        </a>
        , a non-profit open database, for that barcode or search text directly. Their servers see
        the request like any website you visit; nothing about you, your diary or your profile is
        included. The built-in database of 1,400+ foods and all 66 recipes work without any network
        at all.
      </p>
      <h2>Importing from other apps</h2>
      <p>
        CSV files you import from MyFitnessPal, Lose It!, Cronometer or a spreadsheet are read in
        your browser and written to the same local storage. The file is never uploaded.
      </p>
      <h2>Sharing</h2>
      <p>
        Progress and weekly cards are rendered on your device and handed to your phone's share sheet
        or downloaded. A shared plan link encodes your plan settings in the link itself; only the
        people you send it to can read it, and it never includes weigh-ins or diary entries.
      </p>
      <h2>Cookies and tracking</h2>
      <p>
        The site sets no cookies and loads no advertising or social-media scripts. Fonts are
        self-hosted, so no font service sees your visit.
      </p>
      {counting ? (
        <>
          <h2>Anonymous page counts</h2>
          <p>
            To learn which pages and tools help people, the site counts page views with{' '}
            <a href="https://www.goatcounter.com" rel="noopener">
              GoatCounter
            </a>
            , an open-source service that uses no cookies and does not store IP addresses. For each
            page you open, your browser sends the page's address and title, the site that linked you
            here (its domain only), and your screen size. GoatCounter works out the browser,
            operating system and country from the request, and nothing else.
          </p>
          <p>
            Nothing you type, log, weigh or photograph is ever included, and there is no identifier
            that could follow you from one page or day to the next. Counting is off when your
            browser sends Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control, and you can switch it off in
            Settings → Anonymous page counts.
          </p>
        </>
      ) : (
        <p>The site loads no analytics either.</p>
      )}
      <h2>Hosting logs</h2>
      <p>
        Like every website, the hosting provider that serves these files may keep standard,
        short-lived server logs (IP address, requested page, browser type) for security and capacity
        planning. We do not access or analyse them for anything else.
      </p>
      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy ever changes, the update will be recorded on this page with the date. Last
        updated: 30 October 2026.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
